  "question": {
    "text": "Let $E^c$ denotes the complement of an event $E$. Let $E, F, G$ be pairwise independent events with $P(G)>0$ and $P(E \\cap F \\cap G)=0$. Then, $P\\left(E^c \\cap F^c \\mid G\\right)$ equals",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "FZZxV",
      "text": "\n$P\\left(E^c\\right)+P\\left(F^c\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "CzAky",
      "text": "\n$P\\left(E^c\\right)-P\\left(F^c\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "l6j11",
      "text": "\n$P\\left(E^c\\right)-P(F)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "BIhnJ",
      "text": "\n$P(E)-P\\left(F^c\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$$\n\\text { } \\begin{aligned}\nP\\left(\\frac{E^c \\cap F^c}{G}\\right) & =\\frac{P\\left(E^c \\cap F^c \\cap G\\right)}{P(G)} \\\\\n& =\\frac{P(G)-P(E \\cap G)-P(G \\cap F)}{P(G)} \\\\\n& =\\frac{P(G)[1-P(E)-P(F)]}{P(G)} \\\\\n& =1-P(E)-P(F) \\\\\n& =P\\left(E^c\\right)-P(F) .\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
